---
// Lesson 9.8a — The Internet: Packets, Routing & DNS
// expand-app: packets, TCP/UDP, routers, DNS; your GitHub push.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>9.8a — The Internet: Packets, Routing &amp; DNS</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Every <code>git push</code> this semester split your work into
    packets, addressed them, and let strangers' machines forward
    them across the planet. Today: what actually happens in those
    two seconds — the exam's CSN-1 family, told through the command
    you have typed a hundred times.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Packets, not streams.</strong> Nothing crosses the
    internet whole. Your 2 MB scene file becomes ~1,500 chunks
    (packets), each with a header: FROM address, TO address,
    sequence number, checksum. They may take DIFFERENT ROUTES; the
    receiving machine reassembles by sequence number. If a packet
    dies, only it is resent. This is why "the internet routes
    around damage" — a design with no single point of failure:</p>
    <pre><code>your laptop → campus router → ISP → [unknown path] → github.com
                 packet 1 ─────────────────→ arrives
                 packet 2 ─────→ lost → resent, different path → arrives
                 packet 3 ────────────────────────→ arrives</code></pre>

    <p><strong>TCP vs UDP — the reliability trade.</strong></p>
    <table>
        <tr><th></th><th>TCP</th><th>UDP</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Guarantee</td><td>All packets, in order, or none</td><td>Best effort; drops are fine</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Speed cost</td><td>Handshake, receipts, resends</td><td>Fire and forget</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Used for</td><td>git push, web pages, email</td><td>Game packets, video calls, voice</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Your world</td><td>Your repo arrives intact, always</td><td>A shooter's bullet 10 ms late is DROPPED, never awaited</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>That game-vs-git split is the exam's favorite tradeoff: a
    game would rather LOSE a packet than WAIT for one.</p>

    <p><strong>DNS — the phone book.</strong>
    github.com is a name for humans; machines route to IP addresses
    (like 140.82.112.3). DNS is the lookup system that converts,
    hierarchically, with caching at every level. Trace it yourself:</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            Your git push KNOWS github.com but sends to an IP. Walk
            the lookup: your machine → its cache → the campus DNS →
            the root hierarchy → answer cached again. Where is the
            latency (why is the FIRST push slower than the
            second), and why do systems cache aggressively?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — see the route.</strong> In Terminal:</p>
    <pre><code>ping github.com        # DNS happens HERE: name → IP, shown back to you
traceroute github.com  # every router your packets cross (mac: traceroute)</code></pre>
    <p>Count the hops; identify your ISP's boundary (where
    milliseconds jump). Each line is a ROUTER making a local
    decision with no map of the whole path — the "distributed"
    in the exam's phrasing.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — feel the DNS cache.</strong> Ping a site
    twice. First is slower (lookup); second is cached. Now
    <code>sudo dscacheutil -flushcache</code> (or your OS's
    equivalent) and ping again. You just watched cache
    invalidation — the reason "have you tried turning it off and
    on" is real engineering.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — packets, sized right.</strong>
    The exam loves "why ~1,500 bytes?" (the MTU — the largest
    frame most links carry; bigger risks fragmentation everywhere,
    smaller wastes header overhead per byte). Your 2 MB project at
    1,500 bytes ≈ 1,400 packets for a push — count them with
    paper arithmetic. Every push you have made was this exact
    operation.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Network failure modes</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>git push hangs at "resolving deltas." Deltas = the
                packets that changed. Why does a push of ONE
                changed file still involve the WHOLE project's
                history (what is the pack file — and which
                organ's job, network or disk, does the waiting
                happen on)?</li>
            <li>You type gihub.com. What did DNS do — and what did
                your browser do instead (search guess)? Where
                should the typo have failed, and why did it
                not?</li>
            <li>On school wifi your push is fine; at home it times
                out. Which TWO layers do you check first
                (DNS? router? ISP? the site?) — justify the
                order with traceroute evidence.</li>
            <li>The exam classic: "Why is UDP appropriate for a
                multiplayer game but TCP appropriate for
                downloading the game?" Answer with the
                drop-vs-wait trade in one sentence each.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Design for the network</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Design a 2-player co-op turret-defense for the jam
                era: which messages are UDP-worthy (position
                updates — old ones are worthless) and which are
                TCP-worthy (match results)? Label five
                messages.</li>
            <li>What is the LAG-TOLERANT alternative to waiting
                for the server? (Client-side prediction: your
                player moves NOW, server corrects later — why
                does this make your game FEEL 0-lag even at
                80 ms?)</li>
            <li>Hard: your game's leaderboard (someday) posts a
                score of 999999. What must the SERVER do — and
                why is "the client can lie" the single sentence
                of network security (<a href="9.9a.html">9.9a</a>
                bridges here)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain packets, headers, sequence numbers, and reassembly</li>
            <li>Choose TCP vs UDP for a given application with reasons</li>
            <li>Trace DNS resolution and demonstrate the cache</li>
            <li>Read a traceroute and locate a slowdown's layer</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/the-internet.html">The Internet</a> — the full reference</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/index.html">Unit 9: Data &amp; Systems</a> — networks in the CED</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="9.9a.html">9.9a — Encryption: Keys, TLS &amp; Public Key</a></p>
</div>`;
